Thirteen hexagons through each edge
==================================

The full proof is thirteen.tex/thirteen.pdf. The component recovers only the fixed-source lower bound n(4,6,13)>=36 (Table2, physical18, arXiv2401.08271v2). Source authorship is retained. The common Moore/forest/profile/coupled mechanisms are acquired from reviewed components. New seven-vertex and copy-order arguments and exact code deltas are explicit. No exact-count, whole-original, priority, conjecture, Lean or measurement claim is made.

Run normal Python check.py --study STUDY --out FRESH --render. The default performs the full fresh root and completion enumerations, tests the exact old/new block families, positive completions and the literal size guard, and builds the native proof. It requires clang++, 64-bit-word nauty and TeX. The full targets run ordinarily; the two small implementation controls also run with address and undefined-behaviour sanitizers. The root-cover isomorphism library proposes maps only; every reuse requires a literal adjacency bijection.

The earlier unsorted completion reached its 300s internal cap and supplies no coverage. Its source and receipt remain unchanged. Only the sorted complete target execution and the fresh integrated author replay support exclusion. Time or memory failures produce no certificate. Historical source PDFs are bound private inputs; no new sources were intaken.
